### **Program.cs** — stdio transport with DI

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: stdio uses stdout for JSON-RPC — logs must go to stderr
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient();  // registers IHttpClientFactory + HttpClient

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

### **ApiTools.cs** — tool class wrapping a REST API

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Fetches a user profile by ID from the API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The unique user identifier")] string userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        return await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"https://api.example.com/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Searches users by name. Returns JSON array.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Name or partial name to search for")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"https://api.example.com/users?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | How it works |
|-------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class, shown above) | `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, etc. are injected via the primary constructor. The class is resolved from the DI container per invocation. |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services appear as method parameters alongside tool parameters. The SDK distinguishes them automatically — DI services **never** appear in the tool's JSON schema. |

**Key rules:**
- `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`, and any DI-registered service are **auto-injected** and hidden from the LLM
- Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed in the tool schema
- `ILogger<T>` is registered by default — no extra setup
- `AddHttpClient()` registers both `IHttpClientFactory` and `HttpClient`

### Scaffold quickly with the template

```bash
dotnet new install Microsoft.McpServer.ProjectTemplates
dotnet new mcpserver -n MyApiServer
```

Then replace the generated tool class with your `ApiTools.cs` and update `Program.cs` to call `AddHttpClient()`.
